{
  "lead": "When GitHub's request budget runs out, the queue now stops refreshing on its own until the budget refills, instead of firing a refused request every five minutes and on every window activation.",
  "pages": [
    {
      "title": "Overview",
      "summary": "Refreshes now wait until a used-up request budget refills.",
      "widgets": [
        {
          "kind": "section",
          "heading": "A used-up budget now holds the refresh schedule",
          "body": "`RefreshSchedule` already held its timed and activation refreshes back while GitHub asked to slow down (`SlowDown` with a `retry-after`). A used-up budget is a different error, `RateLimited`, and `retry_after` returned `None` for it, so nothing held the schedule:\n\n- the five-minute timer fired and was refused;\n- every window activation older than a minute fired and was refused;\n- each refusal counted as a finished refresh, so the next one came just as soon.\n\nNow `retry_after` also answers for `RateLimited`: the seconds until `resets_at`, the moment GitHub refills the budget."
        },
        {
          "kind": "scope",
          "changes": [
            "A used-up request budget (`RateLimited`) now holds timed and activation refreshes until `resets_at`.",
            "The error message and the hold read the time from one function, `unix_now`."
          ],
          "unchanged": [
            "`SlowDown` keeps its `retry-after` hold, as before.",
            "Errors other than these two give no hold."
          ],
          "mechanical": [
            "`refresh_schedule.rs`: doc comments only."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "tour",
          "title": "Reading order",
          "steps": [
